.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:54: trailing whitespace.
+|---|---|---|---|
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:55: trailing whitespace.
+| DR1 | **High** · failure handling / interface correctness · AC03–05, AC09 | **Recovery cannot establish that the admitted child has stopped using the proposed process interface.** `cli_contract.workflow.verbs.recover` requires confirming both CLI and child are no longer running. `LockOwner` records only the guard PID; `Event` has no child ownership reference. The proposed `run_capture` change adds only `env`, retaining its return-only tuple. In the actual `scripts\run.py:63–97,109–140`, the process handle remains local until completion, and the timeout monitor lives in that same Python process. If the guard is terminated while its child runs, the owner PID can be dead while the child remains active. Clearing the lock can admit overlapping work; refusing forever leaves the advertised recovery unusable. A stale-lock token is not evidence of child termination. | Specify the smallest concrete ownership/recovery seam, including the crash interval around spawning. An unresolved child must remain blocked; do not infer child death from guard death or age. State what evidence permits recovery after an uncertain spawn, without introducing a supervisor service or host API. The existing crash fixture must exercise a **live child**, not only an admission written before spawn. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:56: trailing whitespace.
+| DR2 | **High** · scope correctness / right-sized responsibility · AC03–05 | **The promised detection of undeclared child writes exceeds the specified snapshot boundary.** `algorithms.admission_and_resume` first hashes target/prerequisite scopes, then promises that work/retire commands may change only `allowed_outputs` and that any unexpected source change blocks closure. `InputScope` and `Snapshot` contain only declared inputs; there is no wider before/after inventory. A registered action whose inputs cover `src\a` can also change unrelated `src\b` outside every named scope. Those snapshots cannot distinguish that execution from one changing only `src\a`. This is a routed child, not an arbitrary bypassing tool. | Either define a bounded observed-write inventory sufficient for this promise, or explicitly narrow/remove the undeclared-write policing promise and document that scope completeness is independently reviewed. Do not turn a small admission guard into a filesystem sandbox. Keep required source/check/contract freshness and true blocked-no-spawn guarantees unchanged. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:57: trailing whitespace.
+| DR3 | **Medium** · minimal interfaces / single authority / readability · AC03, AC05, AC09 | **Due-check relationships have several writable authorities without an explicit reconciliation rule.** `Action.requires`, `Check.gates`, and `Increment.due_checks` each describe gating; `algorithms.admission_and_resume` names the first and third but not how the reverse `gates` relation participates. For example, a check names an action in `gates`, while that action omits it from `requires`, and the check is due at increment closure. The contract does not say whether action admission must block, whether closure alone waits, or whether the contract is malformed. This is a concrete handoff ambiguity, amplified by the 69,477-byte specification's separately stored command/replay and graph indexes. Calling it “not a DSL” does not remove the cost of maintaining multiple graph definitions. | Select one authoritative due-edge representation and derive the other views, or define strict equality/consistency validation and the explicit action-versus-closure rule. Prefer deleting redundant writable indexes and deriving replay data from the registered command. Retain only the records needed for the stated negative scenarios; no new graph features are requested. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:58: trailing whitespace.
+| DR4 | **Medium** · canonical consumer consistency · AC04, AC05, AC07, AC09 | **Two operational consumers are missing from the change bindings.** `workflow_document_bindings` does not include `references\delegation.md` or `references\testing-and-e2e.md`. The former's design-review and verification briefs explicitly hand off `design.html`, which this design makes historical after adoption; a fresh verifier can therefore receive the old contract. The latter's “Small test-first cycles” and direct `run.py` suite instructions do not identify the new before-action admission/receipt route. Updating SKILL alone leaves these detailed, actively used briefs inconsistent. This is not a request to rewrite every illustrative HTML link. | Include these two references in the directly affected consumer scope. Resolve the current pointer in delegated briefs; explain when registered checks/handoffs use guard admission and when direct runner execution remains diagnostic/unbound. Keep the existing no-inline independent-review rule and explicit outside-the-guard limitation. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:59: trailing whitespace.
+| DR5 | **Medium** · dependency direction / incremental implementability · AC01, AC02, AC09 | **The promised measurement-first increment lacks an explicit guard-independent persistence boundary.** Both measurement components depend on `workflow_state` snapshot/atomic primitives, while that module also owns workspace loading, adopted contracts and ledger validation. `Snapshot` includes check/action/component/claim bindings. The design names no I1-owned subset or standalone binding mode. Its real ESM/CJS fixture repository must be able to run mutation/probe before `workflow.json`, current-design adoption, receipts and the I2 guard exist. If measurement entry points require that full workspace machinery, I1 cannot independently deliver the P0 fixes; if absent contracts silently become empty bindings, their standalone versus guarded evidentiary meaning is unspecified. This is a boundary omission, not proof that Python cannot implement the helpers in one file. | Establish that standalone measurement hashing/atomic writes and invocation freshness work without loading a workflow ledger or adopted design, and identify their ownership in the measurement-first boundary. A small shared primitive or an explicitly independent I1-owned subset is sufficient; a new module is not mandatory. Later guard validation can consume that evidence without importing future workflow policy into collection. Existing real CLI fixtures should demonstrate this no-workflow-artifacts case. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:60: trailing whitespace.
+| DR6 | **Medium** · grounding / scope control / preservation · AC09 | **Scratch-location changes are justified by a restriction not established by the approved project requirement.** `verification_contract.regression_and_independence.scratch` says existing OS-temp runners “need an explicit project-local scratch seam” to avoid a “workspace restriction”; the mutation CLI safety text also prescribes never using OS temp. Existing `scripts\probe.py:129–147` uses an owned `TemporaryDirectory`, and `evals\lib\score.mjs:115–153` creates a unique private directory and removes that exact owned path. These are not main-checkout writes or broad deletion. The parent's requirement clarification explicitly rejects treating private tool-owned temporary directories as prohibited project behavior and identifies the P0 reproduction's owned-path cleanup. That clarification is attribution, not a new execution by this reviewer. | Correct the asserted rationale and remove mandatory existing-runner changes based solely on it. Project-local scratch may remain a deliberate, proportionate design preference where useful, but not an invented repository-wide safety requirement. Preserve exact ownership and narrowly scoped cleanup wherever scratch lives. No scratch-location refactor is required to fix the four P0 failures. |
.ai/workflow-reliability/design-review.md:61: trailing whitespace.
+| DR7 | **Medium** · testability / host grounding / proportionality · AC06, AC09 | **The held-out exercise adds an access-isolation guarantee beyond the requested blind diagnosis.** `verification_contract.parent_host_experiments.diagnosis` (line 626) requires grader files actually withheld from participant tools and declares the exercise unavailable if that cannot be guaranteed. The parent reports a shared filesystem and no per-path sandbox in the task-agent grant. Under an all-tools-inaccessibility interpretation, the proposed experiment cannot run on that host; separate contexts/directories do not supply that guarantee. Yet neutral participant inputs, withheld cause/oracle and independent post-run grading can support a bounded observed blind exercise without claiming adversarial isolation. | Define blindness in terms of the actual experiment: a neutral seed and participant input/history free of cause, oracle and solution; independent evaluator context; recorded input manifests, observed accesses and access limitations; grading after participant completion. Shared storage must be disclosed, not called isolation. If cause/oracle exposure occurs, or available records cannot substantiate the bounded blindness claim, mark that aspect unverified. If strict filesystem inaccessibility is intentionally retained, name it as an actual host blocker—do not invent a sandbox API. It must not prevent independent P0 implementation/testing; final claims must still disclose any unverified AC06 obligation. |
